win32u: Do not activate a WS_EX_NOACTIVATE window when the active window goes away.

When the active window is hidden or destroyed, activate_other_window()
picks the next visible top-level window, including one created with
WS_EX_NOACTIVATE. Windows documents that style as: "The system does not
bring this window to the foreground when the user minimizes or closes the
foreground window."

Affinity's studio list is a WPF popup (WS_EX_NOACTIVATE). Reordering a
studio drags a ghost window of the row, which takes activation when shown.
When the drop hides it, Wine activated the popup; the window manager then
moved focus on to the main window, and the X FocusOut on the popup, now the
foreground window, sent it WM_CANCELMODE. WPF answers WM_CANCELMODE by
releasing capture, and a popup that loses capture closes. So the list
closed after every reorder.

Skip WS_EX_NOACTIVATE windows in can_activate_window(), which only
activate_other_window() uses.

Checked with real pointer input under a nested KWin: after a reorder the
list stays open, two reorders in a row work, and clicking outside it or on
its button still closes it.

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/window.c b/dlls/win32u/window.c
index 92dc6e6..1293df5 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -4498,6 +4498,9 @@ static BOOL can_activate_window( HWND hwnd )
     if (!(style & WS_VISIBLE)) return FALSE;
     if ((style & (WS_POPUP|WS_CHILD)) == WS_CHILD) return FALSE;
     if (style & WS_MINIMIZE) return FALSE;
+    /* the system does not bring a WS_EX_NOACTIVATE window to the foreground
+     * when the foreground window is closed or hidden */
+    if (get_window_long( hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE ) & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE) return FALSE;
     return !(style & WS_DISABLED);
 }
 
